= Introduction
Compact feature sets matter for activity sensing in life-quality applications, where models run continuously on constrained devices and should be interpretable. Gao et al. [1] select 100 of the 561 UCI HAR features [2] by Boruta filtering and recursive feature elimination (RFE) and report the accuracy cost on unseen participants. We ask: _with the published final configurations and feature list, how much subject-independent accuracy does the compact model lose?_ The paper is recent (October 2026), uses public data, reports this comparison numerically and has no author-released code, so only independent re-implementation can check it.

= Experimental protocol
*Data.* UCI HAR: 30 adults, waist-mounted smartphone, six activities, 2.56~s windows with 50% overlap, 561 features. Official participant-disjoint split: 7,352 training windows (21 participants) and 2,947 test windows (9 participants); archive verified by SHA-256.

*Models.* Python XGBoost 3.2.0 (exact greedy, `base_score` 0.5, one thread), depth 3, $eta = 0.1$, row subsampling 0.8. _Full:_ 561 features, 400 rounds, column subsampling 1.0; _compact:_ the 100 published features, 300 rounds, column subsampling 0.8. Training used only the training partition; each model was tested once. $Delta = "Acc"_"full" - "Acc"_"compact"$ (pp).

*Scope.* Reproduced: both final classifiers and their comparison. Not reproduced: Boruta→RFE with its cross-validation, the hyperparameter search, SHAP.

= Methodological decisions
#dec([D1 · Reproduction scope.],
  [refit the published final configurations on the published feature list.],
  [re-implementing Boruta→RFE and tuning.],
  [unpublished folds and implementation details would add selection variance; the paper's one-standard-error rule chose 100 features at CV accuracy 0.9085 against a threshold of ≈ 0.9084. We therefore do not test whether selection recovers the same subset.])
#dec([D2 · Ambiguous mapping.],
  [the pre-registered first-occurrence (X-axis) column for each of nine `bandsEnergy` names that match three axis-specific columns ($3^9$ = 19,683 mappings; no indices published).],
  [choosing a mapping by test accuracy.],
  [avoids evaluation bias. Y/Z mappings are sensitivity analyses only; X-first is not the authors' confirmed choice.])
#dec([D3 · Uncertainty.],
  [official split, five pre-declared seeds (2026 = reference), paired participant-level bootstrap (9 participants; 10,000 resamples).],
  [one seed with window-level intervals.],
  [windows overlap and cluster within participants; training is stochastic.])

= Results
#figure(kind: table, supplement: [Table],
  caption: [Published [1] vs. reproduced test accuracy (2,947 windows, 9 participants; X-first mapping). The CI covers only the seed-2026 pair; seed variation is the SD.],
  {
    set text(size: 8.3pt)
    set par(justify: false)
    table(columns: (1fr, auto, auto, auto), align: (left, right, right, right), stroke: none,
      inset: (x: 2.6pt, y: 2.1pt),
      table.hline(stroke: 0.8pt),
      table.header([], [*Published*], [*Seed 2026*], [*5 seeds, mean ± SD*]),
      table.hline(stroke: 0.45pt),
      [Full accuracy], [95.55%], [95.25%], [95.44 ± 0.16%],
      [Compact accuracy], [93.89%], [94.16%], [93.70 ± 0.27%],
      [#D (pp)], [+1.66], [+1.09], [+1.74 ± 0.38],
      [95% CI of #D (pp)], [[+0.49, +3.18]], [[+0.29, +2.11]], [—],
      table.hline(stroke: 0.8pt),
    )
  })

*Agreement.* Our pre-registered criterion (reference-seed accuracies within ±1.0 pp of the published values; #D > 0 and within ±1.0 pp of +1.66 pp) classifies the result as _Strong_, as do the five-seed means. The label is descriptive, not an equivalence test.

*Discrepancies.* Published values lie inside our five-seed ranges, and the largest reference-seed deviation (0.57 pp, in #D) is comparable to the seed SD (0.16–0.38 pp). As in the paper, Sitting/Standing confusion dominates compact errors (77 vs. 80 and 29 vs. 29, ours vs. paper), and #D concentrates in three participants (others ≤ +0.62 pp).

*Mapping sensitivity (exploratory).* Y- or Z-axis mappings give mean compact accuracy 93.97% or 94.29% and mean #D +1.47 or +1.15 pp (X-first +1.74 pp); #D > 0 in all 15 compact runs.

= Verification
The protocol predates all model fitting (`ab93dfe1`). From the committed predictions (`be86b6d2`), the #box[`verify-results`] command recomputes every metric, the bootstrap and the decision; 80 tests pass in a fresh clone; re-runs reproduce probabilities bit for bit.

= Limitations
(1) Compact inputs depend on the X-first convention (#D from +1.15 to +1.74 pp across mappings). (2) Five selected pairs are identical columns (95 distinct inputs); SHAP attributions between them are unidentifiable. (3) Python XGBoost 3.2.0 only approximates the authors' R XGBoost 1.7.7.1. (4) #D spans +1.09 to +2.07 pp across seeds. (5) Nine test participants make intervals coarse. (6) Laboratory recordings support no free-living or clinical inference. (7) The configurations also differ in rounds (400 vs. 300) and column subsampling (1.0 vs. 0.8), so #D compares the published configurations, not feature removal alone.

= Next steps
Nest Boruta→RFE inside leave-one-participant-out evaluation over all 30 participants; compare it with RFE alone and a univariate filter; measure feature-set stability (Jaccard overlap); refit both representations with matched hyperparameters; report the per-participant distribution of #D. This tests whether the trade-off generalises across participants and stems from the representation, not the configuration.
